            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house had a significant amount of excess air leakage (heat loss) that was associated with the following locations:



** The shallow heated space below the main floor of the original part of the house. There was ALOT of "colder" air coming up though the furnace ductwork and through the opening grate in the floor beside the wood stove. Although the floor of that lower area appears well sealed, I suspect there are various holes &amp; openings around the perimeter walls (where the foil-faced bubble wrap is pushed up against those walls - but not sealed top, bottom and at the joints). It will be a tedious job, however someone needs to go down there and crawl the full perimeter looking for holes, gaps, etc to fill with caulking or spray foam. The bubble wrap can stay, but needs to be fully sealed top, bottom &amp; the joints. While down there, the floor of this lower area (where the 3" of foam sheets are located) should be looked at closely for holes to fill &amp; the joints of all the sheets of insulation should be sealed with the appropriate type of wide tape. 



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The bathroom fan allows alot of air leakage and should be replaced with a newer Energy Star model that has a good "butterfly" damper to protect against heat loss when it is not in operation.



** The 4" chimney for the current hot water tank allows 4" of "open pipe" for heat loss year round, no matter if the hot water tank is operating or not. This chimney would be eliminated if you replaced this hot water tank with a high efficiency (direct vent) appliance - see the section of my report on the Hot Water System



** There is a 5" combustion air supply pipe on the floor near the furnace. This pipe currently brings in outside combustion air for 1) The furnace (as the new furnace does not have its' own "2-pipe" system direct to the outside, however that model is designed to have this installed), 2) The current hot water tank &amp; 3) The wood stove. If you upgraded the hot water to a high efficiency, direct-vent unit &amp; installed a dedicated outside combustion air pipe directly to the back of the current wood stove, you can eliminate the 5" open combustion air pipe. Even if you reduced the total house air leakage by 40%, you would still have enough air for the furnace to burn inside air (although it would be nice for the furnace to also draw its' combustion air from outside) as well.



Savings: I am modeling a 40% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 750 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 19.7 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house would appear to have R24 batt insulation. In conjunction with your extensive renovation plans, I have modeled the opportunity to strap the ceiling down another 1.5" and replace the existing insulation with R40 ......... either batt insulation or spray foam - whatever you choose.



Savings: By increasing the ceiling insulation from R24 up to a minimum R40, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 151 litre gas hot water tank, with a continuous pilot light &amp; it vents up a 4" chimney. The estimated overall efficiency of this tank is about a 0.55 Energy Factor (EF). As a rough estimate, about 45% of the energy in the gas fuel is being burned by the continuous pilot light or just wasted up the chimney.



Newer houses are installing ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a 0.92 EF or higher (or only about an 8% energy loss to the outside).



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 452 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a newer 92% efficient gas furnace, that draws its' combustion air from inside the house, even though that model is designed to operate on a twin pipe system to the outside. Your woodstove has a 6" chimney and draws its' combustion air from inside the house. In addition, the hot water tank has a 4" chimney and also draws its' combustion air from inside the house.



To satisfy the combustion air requirements of these 3 appliances, there is a 5" open combustion air supply pipe located at floor level beside the furnace. As outlined in the Air Sealing section of this report - your house has a significant amount of natural air leakage (heat loss) and I outlined ways for you to reduce it by about 40%.



In addition to the 40% reduction in air leakage, I would like to see you make the following changes:

1. Have a dedicated outside combustion air brought in &amp; directly sealed to the inlet at the back of your Blaze King wood stove.

2. Upgrade the hot water tank to a high efficiency, direct-vent unit that will eliminate the 4" chimney it uses. See the Hot Water System section of my report for more information.

3. The 5" open combustion air supply pipe can then be eliminated. Even with a 40% reduction in total air leakage, there will still be adequate indoor air available for your furnace to operate in its' current configuration.



Savings: By completing the above 3 changes, you could potentially save $ 570 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 15.0 GJ. These savings are essentially associated with interior heated air being lost to the outside through the current intentional holes for these appliances. Also note these estimated savings are in addition to those outlined in the Air Sealing &amp; Hot Water System sections of this report !!!</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.3 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace is power-vented to the outside and not susceptible to back-drafting, however the hot water tank and wood stove are both chimney vented and are susceptible to back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, there was both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood

        - The bathroom fan

        - The clothes dryer



You may want to consider replacing the current bathroom fan with an Energy Star rated unit, that uses very little electricity, operates at low noise (sone) levels, and will have a good internal damper to seal &amp; prevent excess air leakage heat loss. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Inuvik



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.73 per kWh thereafter

Synthetic Natural Gas (Utilidor Piped) - $37.21/Gigajoule

Oil  - $1.46 per litre 

Propane - $0.69 per litre delivered

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $12.60 per bag, $630.00 per tonne bulk delivered



